6. ATTACKER FRONTIER NEW =E2=80=94 Deadbugz

Primary source:
https://adversa.ai/blog/top-mcp-security-resources-september-2026/
Secondary technical summary:
https://theagentlab.site/blog/2026-09-09-deadbugz-mcp-supply-chain-attack/

Status: *ACTIVE/RECENT campaign* per Adversa AI=E2=80=99s September digest.

Mechanism:

   - malicious MCP server introduced through public GitHub pull requests;
   - outwardly benign tools for text formatting/summarization;
   - initial calls behave as expected;
   - after exactly three tool calls, returned metadata changes;
   - new instructions attempt to cause the consuming agent to search for
   SSH keys, AWS credentials, shell history and Kubernetes config while hid=
ing
   the behaviour.

Reported distribution behaviour:
Adversa says one account submitted 23 PRs across unrelated
AI/developer-tool projects in 74 minutes.

Why technically different:
This explicitly defeats one-time review. The malicious state is temporal
and server-controlled. Tool metadata at approval time is not equivalent to
tool metadata at execution time.

Trust boundary crossed:
remote MCP server -> agent instruction context -> local developer/cloud
credential surface.

Defender implication:
Fingerprint tool metadata and schemas over time; re-evaluate tool outputs
and definitions on reconnect/call; do not grant ambient
filesystem/credential access merely because a server passed initial review.

Privately decision: *BUILD PROBE + CONTENT NOW*.

Safe lab experiment:
Create a benign mock MCP server that returns harmless static metadata for N
calls and then changes one description to request access to a fake secret
file in an isolated fixture. Compare whether candidate gateways detect
metadata drift and/or block the resulting action.

Falsifier:
If mainstream current clients/gateways already continuously hash/revalidate
metadata and enforce file/credential scope by default, this specific wedge
is mostly absorbed.
NEW =E2=80=94 PhantomRaven

Primary source:
https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-us/blog/phantomraven-llm-generated-informati=
on-stealer-for-bug-bounty-hunting/
Published: 2026-09-15

Status: *RECENT real-world supply-chain campaign*.

CrowdStrike reports:

   - JavaScript information stealer distributed through npm;
   - threat actor operated as a bug bounty hunter;
   - dependency-confusion technique used malicious npm packages;
   - CrowdStrike assesses with high confidence that the malware was
   LLM-generated based on code/comment/token-analysis evidence.

Why this matters for Privately:
The mechanism itself is conventional supply-chain compromise, but the
economics have changed: malware authoring and variant generation are
cheaper, and the exact same npm/dependency surface is increasingly
manipulated automatically by coding agents.

Five-year implication:
Attack generation becomes abundant; provenance, package trust, release
integrity and containment become scarcer.

Decision: *CONTENT NOW + BENIGN DEPENDENCY-TRUST TEST*.

Safe experiment:
Provide coding agents a fake package choice where one fixture has
deliberately suspicious provenance metadata but no malicious code. Measure
whether the agent checks ownership, version history, registry/source
consistency and lockfile changes before installation.
